I expected the Pex to generate a test with "ExpectedException" attribute for each method corresponding to possible exceptions in each method. But it is not happening. There is no "ExpectedException" attribute in any of the generated tests. 
Also, I don't see any PexSettings.TargetFrameworks in the generated tests.
What could be wrong or missing?
Regards
I'm still looking for a way to add the "ExpectedException" attribute to tests generated by Pex. Is it possible?
If yes, what am I doing wrong?

A: To add the ExpectedException attribute to tests generated by Pex, you can modify the PexSettings.targets file.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do that:

1. Find the PexSettings.targets file in your Visual Studio installation directory. Typically, it's located at:
   C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\[your_version]\Common7\IDE\Extensions\Microsoft\TestWindow\PexSettings.targets

2. Open the PexSettings.targets file in a text editor.

3. In the target file, look for the TestMethodTags tag in the first line.

4. Modify the TestMethodTags value to include "ExpectedException". For example:
   <TestMethodTags>ExpectedException</TestMethodTags>

5. Save the changes to the PexSettings.targets file.

After you make these changes, when you run the Pex analysis on your code, it should generate tests with the ExpectedException attribute for any possible exceptions in the methods.

Please note that modifying the PexSettings.targets file may impact all analysis in your Visual Studio project, so make sure to save a copy of the original file before making any changes.

I'm sorry for any inconvenience, but I can't add the ExpectedException attribute automatically. If you're still looking for a way to do that, let me know, and I'll see if there's anything else I can help you with.


A: ExpectedExceptions and ExpectedExceptionAttribute are not supported in Pex generated tests as it is mainly used for Unit Testing rather than integration testing or the kind of contract or behavior tests where tests are written to ensure the behavior of the system under test (SUT). ExpectedExceptions were used prior to MSTest 2.0 where Pex was based on it, but they do not have any effect in MSTest 2.0 or later.

Pex generates tests that are focused on code coverage and their main goal is to exercise different parts of the system/product under test. Exceptions are a natural part of software development and are a manifestation of faults in the system.

However, you can check the output produced by Pex and see--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ array after setting x at specified index, contains x 
        }

    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/Test.cs
﻿using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;

public class ArrayListTest
{
    [Test]
    public void CountTest()
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
        list.Add(1);
        list.Add(2);
        list.Add(3);

        Assert.AreEqual(3, list.Count);

        list.Remove(2);

        Assert.AreEqual(2, list.Count);
    }

    [Test]
    public void IndexOfTest()
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
        list.Add(1);
        list.Add(2);
        list.Add(3);

        Assert.AreEqual(1, list.IndexOf(2));
        Assert.AreEqual(-1, list.IndexOf(4));
    }

    [Test]
    public void ContainsTest()
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
        list.Add(1);
        list.Add(2);
        list.Add(3);

        Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(2));
        Assert.IsFalse(list.Contains(4));
    }
}

//PexApiWrapper/ArrayList.cs
﻿namespace PexAPIMock
{
    using System;
    using System.Collections;
    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using System.Linq;
    using Contracts;

    public class ArrayList : IListContract<int>
    {
        private List<int> list;

        public ArrayList()
        {
            this.list = new List<int>();
        }

        public bool Contains(int item)
        {
            return this.list.Contains(item);
        }

        public int Add(int item)
        {
            this.list.Add(item);
            return this.list.Count;
        }

        public void Remove(int item)
        {
            this.list.Remove(item);
        }

        public int IndexOf(int item)
        {
            return this.list.IndexOf(item);
        }

        public void Insert(int index, int item)
        {
            this.list.Insert(index, item);
        }

        public int this[int index]
        {
            get
            {
                return this.list[index];
            }

            set
            {
                this.list[index] = value;
            }
        }
    }
}

//PexApiWrapper/Contracts/IListContract.cs
﻿namespace PexAPIMock.Contracts
{
    using System;

    public interface IListContract<T>
    {
        bool Contains(T item);

        int Add(T item);

        void Remove(T item);

        int IndexOf(T item);

        void Insert(int index, T item);

        T this[int index] { get; set; }
    }
